\section{Knots}{

The spline needs \code{df + 1} knots: two boundary knots and \code{df - 1}
interior ones. By default they are placed at equally spaced quantiles
of the log UNCENSORED times, which is Royston and Parmar's own rule
and flexsurv's default; the boundary knots are then the smallest and
largest log uncensored time.

The quantiles cannot be taken until the response is in hand, so they
are taken at frame assembly through \code{family_finalize()}, and the
family object the fit carries has its knots baked in. \verb{knots =} and
\verb{bknots =} pin them instead, in which case they are taken as given
and no data is consulted. Both are on the LOG time scale, as
flexsurv's are.
}

\section{Monotonicity is not enforced, and the floor is not free}{

The cumulative hazard has to increase, which means the spline's
derivative in \code{x} has to stay positive, and nothing in this
parameterization holds it there. flexsurv does not hold it either:
the model is fitted unconstrained and a fit whose spline turns over
inside the data range is over-parameterized, not something the
software should have prevented.

What this family does differently is refuse to return \code{NaN} for it.
Where the derivative goes non-positive there is no hazard and the
true log density is \code{-Inf}, so the log density becomes a large finite
negative number instead: \code{NaN} stops the optimizer, and inside a
\code{\link[frmtmb:mixture]{frmtmb::mixture()}} one \code{NaN} component poisons the log-sum-exp of
all of them.

That floor is NOT inert when it is used. It keeps the fit alive and
it makes \code{logLik()} and \code{AIC()} a pseudo-likelihood rather than a
density: a 60 percent cure-fraction dataset has been measured
converging, without a warning, with 6 floored rows and a reported log
likelihood 3952 units away from the density's. Nothing in the fitted
object says so, because the family protocol has no hook that runs
when a fit finishes.

\code{\link[=rp_floored]{rp_floored()}} is where that goes to be read, and it REFUSES by
default. Call it on every fit. \code{frm_curve()} and its two companions
call it for you, because the fitted curve of a floored fit is a floor
artifact too.
}

\section{Censoring, truncation, and the accuracy limit on log S}{

The family declares both a density and a distribution function, so
\code{cens()} and \code{trunc()} both work, and right, left and interval
censoring all reach the likelihood through frmtmb's own machinery.
\code{cens()} takes frmtmb's coding: \code{0} (or \code{"none"}) is an observed
event and \code{1} (or \code{"right"}) is right censored, which is the OPPOSITE
of the \code{status} column of a \code{Surv()} object. Pass \code{1 - status}.

Both are CONDITIONAL rather than unqualified. frmtmb forms a
right-censored contribution as \code{log(1 - F(y))} on the PROBABILITY
scale (\code{R/objective.R:100}), and core offers a family no
complementary log-CDF slot to hand back \verb{log S} directly, so the
scored \verb{log S} carries absolute error about
\code{.Machine$double.eps / S} whatever this family does internally.
Measured, by forming \code{F} for a given \verb{-log S} and reading
\code{log(1 - F)} back (the three scales agree to every printed digit):

\tabular{lll}{
\verb{-log S} \tab computed \tab absolute error \cr
10   \tab -10        \tab 1.3e-13 \cr
19.2 \tab -19.2      \tab 2.4e-10 \cr
30   \tab -29.99983  \tab 1.7e-04 \cr
36   \tab -34.94504  \tab 1.05    \cr
40   \tab -35.12736  \tab 4.87
}

\verb{log S} is floored at -35.127363 whatever the model says, and past
\verb{-log S} of 30 the term is FLAT: its gradient is exactly zero, so the
optimizer prices that row at a constant and fits the rest as if it
were free.

How wrong the answer gets is a property of the DATA, not of the
family. A floored censored row contributes -35.127363 instead of its
own \verb{-log S}, so the reported log likelihood is short by about
\verb{-log S - 35} for each such row. That is thousands to tens of
thousands as soon as one censored time sits well past the event
times: two runs of the same 600-subject design, differing only in
seed, give 2.4e+03 and 2.166e+04. Both converged without a warning
and both put the treatment coefficient out by tens of percent, on
data flexsurv declines to fit at all.

\verb{-log S} is the cumulative hazard \code{H} on the \code{"hazard"} scale,
\code{log(1 + exp(eta))} on \code{"odds"} and \code{-log(Phi(-eta))} on \code{"normal"}.
\code{\link[=rp_floored]{rp_floored()}} checks it on every censored row and refuses past 19.2,
which is where \code{eps / S} passes 1e-8. The real fix is a core one, an
\code{lccdf} slot, and it is in \code{dev/spline-seam-proposal.md}.
}

